{"maxId":109,"entries":{"chapter-105-study-bacterial-pneumonia-and-tuberculosis|Chapter 105 Study: Bacterial Pneumonia and Tuberculosis":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-epidemiology|Definition & Epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|Pneumonia (clinical definition) = symptoms (fever, chills, cough, pleuritic pain) + signs (hyper-/hypothermia, raised RR, dullness, crackles, wheezing, pleural rub) + an opacity on chest x-ray; research definitions require ≥2 symptoms, ≥1 sign, and a new opacity not due to other conditions (p1679).":["u4"],"copy|Incidence 2-10 cases/1000/yr overall; in patients >65, European/US registries report 25-40/1000/yr with 7%-35% mortality (p1680).":["u5"],"copy|US burden: >4 million annual outpatient visits, >1 million hospitalizations/yr (600,000 in adults >65); 10-20% of hospitalized patients need ICU; mean length of stay 5 days; overall 30-day mortality 23% (p1680).":["u6"],"copy|Mortality 1%-2% in young patients without comorbidity vs 25%-50% in older adults with severe comorbidity; 30-day mortality 3x higher in patients >85 vs 65-74; >50,000 US deaths/yr, 85% in patients >65; 30-day readmission ~20%; annual US cost >$17 billion, 80% driven by hospitalization (p1680).":["u7"],"etiology-amp-resistant-organism-risk|Etiology & Resistant-Organism Risk":["u8"],"copy|Streptococcus pneumoniae remains the single most common agent, 20%-65% of cases (p1681).":["u9"],"copy|Viruses implicated in 12%-18% of cases; influenza, RSV, and human metapneumovirus together account for 8%-14% (p1681).":["u10"],"copy|Hospital-acquired pneumonia begins at least 48 h after admission and was not incubating on arrival; Hazzard also discusses recent discharge (10–14 days) as a healthcare exposure, which alone does not define HAP (p1681).":["u11"],"copy|HAP pathogen-specific risk factors (Table 105-1, p1681): MDR pathogens — prior antibiotics, hospitalization >5 days in the prior 90 d, high hospital resistance rate, nursing-home residence, home IV therapy, chronic dialysis, active wound care, contact with an MDR-infected patient, or immunosuppressive disease/therapy; Pseudomonas/MDR bacilli — recent ICU admission, prior antibiotics, chronic corticosteroids, structural lung disease, or late-onset nosocomial pneumonia; Legionella — chronic corticosteroids, hematologic cancer, contaminated-water contact, or prior nosocomial Legionella cases; S aureus — recent ICU admission, prior influenza, colonization/prevalence of methicillin-resistant strains, TBI, coma, diabetes, or renal failure; anaerobes — poor oral hygiene, decreased consciousness, airway manipulation, or recent abdominal surgery.":["u12"],"copy|Shorr MDRO risk score: recent hospitalization 4 pts, admission from long-term care 3 pts, hemodialysis 2 pts, critical illness 1 pt; a score of 0 carries 84% negative predictive value for MDRO (p1682).":["u13"],"copy|One study: clinical signs of severe pneumonia plus two risk factors (immunosuppression, hospitalization in the prior 90 d, Barthel <50, antibiotics in the prior 6 mo) were associated with higher MDRO frequency (2% vs 27%). Ewig / Brito-Niederman approach: ≥2 multidrug-resistance factors (severe pneumonia, hospitalization in the prior 90 d, nursing-home residence, severe basal dependence, immunosuppression, antibiotics in the prior 6 mo) → empiric MDRO coverage warranted (p1682).":["u14"],"copy|ESBL-producing Klebsiella found in 20% of US and 35% of European pneumonia isolates (p1683).":["u15"],"copy|Pseudomonas is uncommon in older adults overall (1%-2%), but should be suspected with severe COPD (FEV1 <35%), multiple prior antibiotic courses, ICU admission and/or bronchiectasis, or prior colonization (p1683).":["u16"],"copy|Risk factors for less-common pathogens (Table 105-2, p1682): ESBL-producing Enterobacteriaceae — prior antibiotics, hemodialysis, long-term indwelling urinary catheter, long-stay-center residence, recent hospitalization, diabetes, or recurrent UTIs; aspiration-prone Enterobacteriaceae/anaerobes — functional impairment, dysphagia, GERD, history of vomiting, cerebrovascular disease, dementia, periodontal disease, or poor oral hygiene; MRSA — wound/bedsore care, serious illness plus recent hospitalization plus prior IV antibiotics plus institutionalization, previous colonization, or superinfection during an influenza epidemic.":["u17"],"copy|Exposure-specific pathogens (Table 105-3, p1683): bird/fowl/farm-animal contact → Chlamydophila psittaci; rabbit contact → Coxiella burnetii; bat contact or residence in an endemic area → Histoplasma capsulatum; air-conditioning/cooling-tower exposure → Legionella pneumophila; travel to Southeast Asia → Burkholderia pseudomallei, avian influenza; alcohol intoxication → S pneumoniae, K pneumoniae, anaerobes, S aureus; prison inmates → S pneumoniae, M tuberculosis; advanced HIV → S pneumoniae, H influenzae, P jiroveci, M tuberculosis.":["u18"],"copy|Shorr MRSA prediction score: low risk if total score ≤1 (p1683).":["u19"],"diagnosis-amp-severity-stratification|Diagnosis & Severity Stratification":["u20"],"copy|Chest x-ray can be falsely negative in up to 30% of cases (more so with dehydration/neutropenia); repeat the film at 24-48 h if the first is negative and suspicion remains (p1684).":["u21"],"copy|Serum lactate ≥2 mmol/L predicts 30-day mortality (p1685).":["u22"],"copy|Pneumococcal urinary antigen: sensitivity >60%, specificity >90%; stays positive for ~3 months after resolution, limiting its use to judge relapse or treatment response (p1685).":["u23"],"copy|Legionella urinary antigen detects only serogroup I but has sensitivity >90% for that serogroup (p1685).":["u24"],"copy|Admission criteria: PSI ≥ III or CURB-65 ≥ 2; a short-stay unit is a safe, cost-effective alternative for patients with CURB-65 of 2 or PSI of III (p1687).":["u25"],"copy|CURB-65 scoring (Table 105-4, p1686): 1 pt each for confusion, urea >44 mg/dL (BUN >20), RR ≥30, SBP <90 or DBP ≤60, age ≥65; mortality by score — 0-1 low (0.7%-2.1%, consider outpatient), 2 intermediate (9.2%), 3 high (15%, admit), 4-5 >40% (admit, consider ICU).":["u26"],"copy|SCAP severity score (Table 105-5, p1686): major criteria — pH <7.30 (13 pts), SBP <90 mm Hg (11 pts); minor criteria — confusion (5 pts), BUN >30 mg/dL (5 pts), RR >30 (9 pts), multilobar/bilateral CXR (5 pts), PaO2 <54 mm Hg or PaO2/FiO2 <250 (6 pts), age ≥80 (5 pts); risk — low 0-1 (<10 pts), intermediate 2 (10-19 pts), high 3-4 (20 points or more).":["u27"],"copy|SMART-COP score (Table 105-6, p1687): SBP <90 (2 pts), multilobar involvement (1 pt), albumin <3.5 g/dL (1 pt), RR age-adjusted (1 pt: ≤50y ≥25/min, >50y ≥30/min), tachycardia ≥125 (1 pt), confusion (1 pt), oxygenation age-adjusted (2 pts: ≤50y PaO2 <70 or SpO2 ≤93% or PaO2/FiO2 <333; >50y PaO2 <60 or SpO2 ≤90% or PaO2/FiO2 <250), pH <7.35 (2 pts); risk for needing vasopressor/intensive respiratory support — 0-2 low, 3-4 moderate, 5-6 high, ≥7 very high (Charles et al., Clin Infect Dis 2008; the original score places age 50 in the younger group).":["u28"],"copy|Discharge readiness: HR <100 bpm, RR <24/min, axillary temperature <37.2°C (<98.96°F), systolic BP >90 mm Hg, SpO2 >90%, plus baseline awareness and oral tolerance; most patients stabilize by hospital day 3-4, up to 7 days if frail (p1687).":["u29"],"management-framework-amp-treatment|Management Framework & Treatment":["u30"],"copy|Two questions should drive antibiotic choice: (1) severe pneumonia or risk factors for unusual pathogens? (2) does the patient meet criteria for frailty? Both \"no\" → standard younger-adult regimens; severe pneumonia and/or risk factors for less common pathogens → consider broadening; mildly frail → same antibiotics as independent patients plus functional/cognitive monitoring; moderate-severe frailty → broader coverage in addition to routine pathogens (p1687-1689).":["u31"],"copy|CAP treatment tiers (Table 105-8, p1688): non-frail patients get cephalosporin or amoxicillin/clavulanate + azithromycin/fluoroquinolone, escalating in severe disease to ± linezolid/vancomycin, ± antipseudomonal agent, ± oseltamivir; prefrail patients get amoxicillin/clavulanate or ceftriaxone/cefotaxime/ceftaroline + azithromycin/fluoroquinolone; frail patients get a different regimen, not the same combo broadened — ertapenem, or amoxicillin/clavulanate, or ceftriaxone + clindamycin.":["u32"],"copy|Quinolones should not be prescribed when pulmonary tuberculosis is suspected, because of their tuberculostatic activity and the possibility of delaying the TB diagnosis (p1689).":["u33"],"copy|Treatment failure = no clinical stability by 3-4 days, or deterioration/respiratory failure/septic shock in the first 72 h; associated with a fivefold increase in mortality. However, stability may take longer without implying therapeutic failure in older patients with severe pneumonia or serious comorbidity (eg, severe COPD, untreated heart failure) (p1690).":["u34"],"copy|A 5-7 day course is sufficient for most pneumonia even in older adults; extend for Legionella, resistant GNB, or MRSA, or for persistent fever beyond 72 h, persistence of >1 instability criterion, inadequate initial coverage, or complications (p1690).":["u35"],"copy|PCV13 should be given first, with PPSV given at least 8 weeks later, per ACIP (p1690).Current practice (not the book): prior vaccination determines the schedule: PCV15, PCV20 or PCV21 for adults ≥50 who never received a PCV or whose history is unknown; after PCV15 give PPSV23 if not previously administered, one year later (8 weeks for certain conditions); no PPSV23 after PCV20/PCV21 — CDC, 2026.":["u36"],"tuberculosis-epidemiology-amp-pathophysiology|Tuberculosis — Epidemiology & Pathophysiology":["u37"],"copy|>1.7 billion people (about 25% of the world's population) are estimated to be infected with M tuberculosis; high-burden regions run ≥100/100,000, while the US, Western Europe, Canada, Japan, and Australia run <25/100,000 (p1691).":["u38"],"copy|TB incidence is bimodal: one peak at 15-35 years, a second after 65 years (p1691).":["u39"],"copy|Healthy people infected with TB carry a 5%-10% lifetime risk of active disease; 50%-80% of those who progress do so within the first 2 years (p1692).":["u40"],"copy|HIV coinfection without antiretroviral treatment carries a 10% annual risk of active disease (p1692).":["u41"],"tuberculosis-diagnosis-amp-treatment|Tuberculosis — Diagnosis & Treatment":["u42"],"copy|Two-step tuberculin test for initial geriatric screening (older adults have increased cutaneous anergy): a negative first test (induration <10 mm) is followed by a repeat test at 2 weeks; positive = ≥10 mm and an increase of ≥6 mm over the first test (p1693).":["u43"],"copy|Isoniazid causes hepatotoxicity in up to 4%-5% of patients >65 y, versus <1% in those <65 y (p1694).":["u44"],"copy|Chemoprophylaxis is generally reserved for a Mantoux induration increase ≥15 mm over a prior negative test; in close contacts of active TB and other high-risk individuals with negative TST or IGRA, preventive treatment should also be considered, unless contraindicated (p1694).":["u45"],"copy|For latent TB, short rifamycin-based regimens are preferred when suitable, including rifampin 4 months or isoniazid plus rifapentine 12 weeks; isoniazid 9 months is an alternative when rifamycins are unsuitable (p1694; NTCA/CDC latent tuberculosis treatment guideline, 2020).":["u46"],"copy|Standard active-TB regimen: 4-drug intensive phase 2 months, then 2-drug continuation phase ≥4 months (total 6 months); extend the continuation phase to 7 months (9 months total) for cavitary disease plus a positive culture at month 2, or if the intensive phase omitted pyrazinamide (p1694).":["u47"],"copy|Drug-resistant TB varies with the resistance pattern; the book describes older 18–24 month regimens, but current guidance recommends six-month regimens for eligible adults with rifampin-resistant pulmonary TB, selected according to susceptibility and treatment history (p1694; ATS/CDC/ERS/IDSA guideline update, 2025).":["u48"],"copy|First-line regimen exceptions (Table 105-12, p1695): give pyridoxine 10-50 mg/day with isoniazid if alcoholic, malnourished, diabetic, or has kidney failure (peripheral neuropathy risk); if isoniazid or rifampin can't be used (toxicity/resistance), substitution regimens with the remaining first-line drugs ± moxifloxacin exist — it isn't an automatic jump to second-line/MDR therapy.":["u49"],"copy|Asymptomatic AST elevation occurs in ~20% of patients on the standard 4-drug regimen and usually resolves spontaneously (p1696).":["u50"],"copy|Stop hepatotoxic TB drugs if bilirubin ≥3 mg/dL or transaminases >5x the upper limit of normal (or >3x ULN with symptoms of hepatitis) (p1696).":["u51"],"recall|Recall":["u52"],"copy|CHAPTER 105s / HAZZARD":["u53"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 105; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u54"],"copy|Exam trap: a quinolone looks like a reasonable empiric choice for undifferentiated CAP, but the book says not to prescribe it when pulmonary TB is suspected — its tuberculostatic activity can delay the TB diagnosis (p1689).":["u55"],"copy|2.":["u56"],"copy|What four items make up the Shorr MDRO risk score, and what does a score of zero mean?":["u57"],"copy|Recent hospitalization (4 pts), admission from long-term care (3 pts), hemodialysis (2 pts), critical illness (1 pt); a score of 0 carries an 84% negative predictive value for MDRO (p1682).":["u58"],"copy|3.":["u59"],"copy|What rates of ESBL-producing Klebsiella were found among pneumonia isolates in the US vs Europe?":["u60"],"copy|20% (US) vs 35% (Europe) (p1683).":["u61"],"copy|4.":["u62"],"copy|In what fraction of cases can the initial chest x-ray be falsely negative, and what should be done if suspicion for pneumonia remains?":["u63"],"copy|Up to 30% (more often with dehydration or neutropenia); repeat the film at 24-48 hours (p1684).":["u64"],"copy|6.":["u65"],"copy|What are the sensitivity/specificity of the pneumococcal urinary antigen test, and what limits its use in relapse or treatment-response assessment?":["u66"],"copy|Sensitivity >60%, specificity >90%; it stays positive for about 3 months after resolution of the pneumonia (p1685).":["u67"],"copy|7.":["u68"],"copy|What PSI and CURB-65 thresholds define criteria for hospital admission?":["u69"],"copy|PSI ≥ III or CURB-65 ≥ 2 (p1687).":["u70"],"copy|8.":["u71"],"copy|What vital-sign criteria define readiness for hospital discharge, and by what day do most patients stabilize?":["u72"],"copy|HR <100 bpm, RR <24/min, axillary temperature <37.2°C (<98.96°F), systolic BP >90 mm Hg, SpO2 >90%, good awareness and oral tolerance; most stabilize by hospital day 3-4 (up to 7 days if frail) (p1687).":["u73"],"copy|9.":["u74"],"copy|How is antibiotic treatment failure defined, and what mortality risk does it carry?":["u75"],"copy|No clinical stability by 3-4 days, or deterioration/respiratory failure/septic shock in the first 72 hours; associated with a fivefold increase in mortality.":["u76"],"copy|But in older patients with severe pneumonia or serious comorbidity (eg, severe COPD, untreated heart failure), stability may take longer without implying therapeutic failure (p1690).":["u77"],"copy|10.":["u78"],"copy|What duration of antibiotic therapy is sufficient for most older adults with pneumonia, and when should it be extended?":["u79"],"copy|5-7 days; extend for infection by Legionella, resistant gram-negative bacilli, or MRSA, or for persistent fever beyond 72 hours, persistence of more than one criterion of clinical instability, inadequate initial coverage, or complications (p1690).":["u80"],"copy|12.":["u81"],"copy|Roughly what fraction of the world's population is estimated to be infected with M tuberculosis, and what is the bimodal age pattern of TB incidence?":["u82"],"copy|About 25% (>1.7 billion people); incidence peaks at 15-35 years and again after 65 years (p1691).":["u83"],"copy|13.":["u84"],"copy|What is the two-step tuberculin test protocol in older adults, and why is it used?":["u85"],"copy|Older adults have increased cutaneous anergy; if the first TST is negative (induration <10 mm), a second test is done at 2 weeks.":["u86"],"copy|A positive result is induration ≥10 mm and an increase of ≥6 mm over the first test (p1693).":["u87"],"copy|14.":["u88"],"copy|What isoniazid hepatotoxicity rate is seen in patients over 65, compared with those under 65?":["u89"],"copy|Up to 4%-5% in patients >65 years, versus <1% in those <65 years (p1694).":["u90"],"copy|15.":["u91"],"copy|At what bilirubin or transaminase thresholds should hepatotoxic TB drugs be stopped?":["u92"],"copy|Bilirubin ≥3 mg/dL, or transaminases >5x the upper limit of normal (>3x ULN if the patient has symptoms of hepatitis) (p1696).":["u93"],"copy|Answer":["u94","u95","u96","u97","u98","u99","u100","u101","u102","u103","u104","u105"],"copy|Hazzard defines hospital-acquired pneumonia as pneumonia arising more than 48 hours into a hospital stay or during the 10–14 days after hospital discharge (p1681).Current practice (not the book): ATS/IDSA defines HAP as pneumonia arising ≥48 hours after admission that was not incubating on admission. Recent discharge alone does not meet that definition — ATS/IDSA HAP/VAP guideline, 2016.":["u11"],"copy|Hazzard usually treats latent TB with 9 months of isoniazid unless resistance is suspected. 4 months of rifampin is the alternative for isoniazid resistance or intolerance, and 12 weeks of isoniazid plus rifapentine was also effective (p1694).Current practice (not the book): when suitable, short rifamycin-based courses are preferred, including 3 months of weekly isoniazid plus rifapentine or 4 months of daily rifampin. Daily isoniazid for 6 or 9 months is an alternative — NTCA/CDC guideline, 2020.":["u46"],"copy|CHAPTER 105 / HAZZARD":["u53"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[110,-4],[114,0]]}
